Description: Headless Chromium renderer for CARLOS EMR eForm PDFs
 Saved-eForm print, fax and archive in CARLOS are produced by rendering the
 form in a real browser and printing it to PDF over the Chrome DevTools
 Protocol. There is no legacy fallback path: without a browser those three
 workflows are unavailable.
 .
 Ubuntu 26.04 ships Chromium only as a snap, and a confined snap cannot read
 the renderer's working directories under /var/lib/carlos-emr. This package
 therefore installs a pinned, unconfined Chromium and a chromedriver built from
 the same revision into /usr/lib/carlos-emr/chromium, runs the driver as the
 carlos-emr-chromedriver service, and points eform_pdf_browser_service_url
 (plus eform_pdf_browser_chromium_path for the browser binary) at it, so the
 application connects to a running service and never downloads a driver.
 .
 The browser runs as its own system account (carlos-render, a member of no
 group) under its own systemd unit, carlos-emr-chromedriver. That unit is the
 only place on the host where namespace creation is permitted, which is what
 lets Chromium use its own sandbox: the EMR's service keeps namespaces denied,
 and a browser the JVM spawned would inherit that and be unable to sandbox
 itself. The application connects to the browser over loopback and never
 launches one.
 .
 Installing this package also turns the boot-time renderer probe back on and
 enables Chromium's sandbox, so a broken browser is reported once in the journal
 at startup instead of surfacing as a failed print in the middle of a
 consultation.
